  <static seed="187" source="Library/ASU-topics/setProductQuotientRule/3-5-70.pg">
    <statement><p>One hour after <m>x</m> milligrams of a drug are given to a person, the
change in body temperature <m>T(x)</m> (in degrees fahrenheit)
is given by the function
 <me>T(x) = x^2 (1 - \frac{x}{9}).</me>
Further, the rate <m>T'(x)</m> at which <m>T</m> changes with respect to
the size of the dosage, <m>x</m>, is called the sensitivity
of the body to the dosage.  Find:</p>
<p>(A) <m>T'(1)</m> = <fillin characters="40" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p>(B) <m>T'(3)</m> = <fillin characters="40" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p>(C) <m>T'(6)</m> = <fillin characters="40" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>2\cdot \left(1-\frac{1}{9}\right)+\frac{-1}{9}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>6\cdot \left(1-\frac{3}{9}\right)+9\cdot \frac{-1}{9}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>12\cdot \left(1-\frac{6}{9}\right)+36\cdot \frac{-1}{9}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
